{
	"cell_id": 5825391031596479778,
	"cells": [
		{
			"cell_id": 434957331039847406,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 8457162150275920288,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\section*{The Kerr spacetime}\n\nThis notebook computes the Christoffel symbols, the Riemann tensor, the Ricci\ntensor and the Kretschmann scalar of the Kerr metric, in Boyer-Lindquist\ncoordinates. It follows the same steps as the Schwarzschild example, with one\nchange in how the components are simplified, explained below.\n\nWe start by declaring the coordinates, the indices and the metric."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "\\section*{The Kerr spacetime}\n\nThis notebook computes the Christoffel symbols, the Riemann tensor, the Ricci\ntensor and the Kretschmann scalar of the Kerr metric, in Boyer-Lindquist\ncoordinates. It follows the same steps as the Schwarzschild example, with one\nchange in how the components are simplified, explained below.\n\nWe start by declaring the coordinates, the indices and the metric."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 5505063739871462623,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "input",
			"source": "__cdbkernel__.display_fractions=True"
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 5783191108520599581,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "input",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 890090922588184480,
					"cell_origin": "server",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\begin{dmath*}{}\\text{Property Coordinate attached to~}\\left[t, \\discretionary{}{}{}r, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\theta, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\phi\\right].\\end{dmath*}"
				},
				{
					"cell_id": 12188803854052835094,
					"cell_origin": "server",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\begin{dmath*}{}\\text{Property Indices(position=fixed, values=\\{{t}\n, {r}\n, {\\theta}\n, {\\phi}\n\\}) attached to~}\\left[\\mu, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\nu, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\rho, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\sigma, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\lambda, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\kappa, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\chi, \\discretionary{}{}{}\\gamma\\right].\\end{dmath*}"
				},
				{
					"cell_id": 16780564516974431719,
					"cell_origin": "server",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\begin{dmath*}{}\\text{Property PartialDerivative attached to~}\\backslash\\texttt{partial}\\{\\#\\}.\\end{dmath*}"
				}
			],
			"source": "{t,r,\\theta,\\phi}::Coordinate;\n{\\mu,\\nu,\\rho,\\sigma,\\lambda,\\kappa,\\chi,\\gamma}::Indices(values={t,r,\\theta,\\phi}, position=fixed);\n\\partial{#}::PartialDerivative;\ng_{\\mu\\nu}::Metric.\ng^{\\mu\\nu}::InverseMetric."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 4935032118749359560,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 4670163865567620684,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "For the evaluation of components the scalar backend (Sympy) needs to see all\ndependence on the coordinates explicitly. Rather than introducing the usual\nabbreviations $\\Sigma = r^2 + a^2\\cos^2\\theta$ and $\\Delta = r^2 - 2Mr + a^2$\nas abstract functions, we therefore write the metric components out in full."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "For the evaluation of components the scalar backend (Sympy) needs to see all\ndependence on the coordinates explicitly. Rather than introducing the usual\nabbreviations $\\Sigma = r^2 + a^2\\cos^2\\theta$ and $\\Delta = r^2 - 2Mr + a^2$\nas abstract functions, we therefore write the metric components out in full."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 5874921186984711181,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "input",
			"source": "ss:= { g_{t t} = -(1-2 M r/(r**2+a**2 \\cos(\\theta)**2)),\n       g_{t \\phi} = -2 M a r \\sin(\\theta)**2/(r**2+a**2 \\cos(\\theta)**2),\n       g_{\\phi t} = -2 M a r \\sin(\\theta)**2/(r**2+a**2 \\cos(\\theta)**2),\n       g_{r r} = (r**2+a**2 \\cos(\\theta)**2)/(r**2-2 M r+a**2),\n       g_{\\theta\\theta} = r**2+a**2 \\cos(\\theta)**2,\n       g_{\\phi\\phi} = (r**2+a**2\n                       +2 M a**2 r \\sin(\\theta)**2/(r**2+a**2 \\cos(\\theta)**2))\n                      \\sin(\\theta)**2 }."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 4617654160955044332,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 6816152149612795223,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "By default, \\algo{evaluate} runs Sympy's \\verb|simplify| on every component\nafter every intermediate step. That works well for Schwarzschild, but for Kerr\nit is very slow, and its running time varies a lot from run to run (Sympy's\nmultivariate factorisation picks random evaluation points, and some choices\nare very expensive).\n\nSince all components are rational functions of $r$, $\\cos\\theta$ and\n$\\sin\\theta$, there is a cheaper and fully reliable alternative: the\n\\verb|trigrat| function from the \\verb|cdb.core.trigrat| package brings such\nexpressions into a canonical form, so that a component which vanishes is always\nrecognised as zero, and removes the vanishing components."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "By default, \\algo{evaluate} runs Sympy's \\verb|simplify| on every component\nafter every intermediate step. That works well for Schwarzschild, but for Kerr\nit is very slow, and its running time varies a lot from run to run (Sympy's\nmultivariate factorisation picks random evaluation points, and some choices\nare very expensive).\n\nSince all components are rational functions of $r$, $\\cos\\theta$ and\n$\\sin\\theta$, there is a cheaper and fully reliable alternative: the\n\\verb|trigrat| function from the \\verb|cdb.core.trigrat| package brings such\nexpressions into a canonical form, so that a component which vanishes is always\nrecognised as zero, and removes the vanishing components."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 7528743387346597307,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "input",
			"source": "from cdb.core.trigrat import trigrat"
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 1793317786863289084,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
